  <h1 class="theme-title">Empty House</h1>
  <div class="theme-body">
    <p>I always tell myself that it could be worse, but truthfully I am not sure it could be better either.</p>
    <p>One of the hardest pills I've had to swallow in my adulthood it's the concept of things drifting apart from me, it's a slow process, almost torture like. I have always thought of myself, and humans in general really, as something tangible, but it's kinda ridiculous for me to keep thinking that.</p>
    <p>Stare into someone for just one minute, in the eyes, I'm sure you will see it too. We are built from nothing, collapsible in an instant. We are elements of natures arranged, atoms created and decaying. I thought we were tangible but we really are just dust waiting, our insides made of flickering lights.</p>
    <p>I've always considered myself somewhat too attached with the idea of love, whether it's friendship or romantic love, and especially the idea of controlling it. The concept that no matter what I could always fight for a specific relationship has by now calcified into my identity; it's extremely hard for me to let things go, which is weird cause it's not that I hate change, I love change and have always welcomed it, and yet I have this adversion to my relationships not going how I plan them to go.</p>
    <p>It's not the type of "straight man control" either yk? It's just this overwhelming feeling that manifests in me not being able to put down the weight of maintaining xyz type or relationship, I don't trust anyone (except Sara hi biiitch ik you are never reading this cuz u don't read but that's ok ily so muchhhh) to be able to carry the weight with me, were I to put it down.</p>
    <p>These aren't, however, character traits, they're adaptations, there is a difference and recognising it is the first crack of light. I mean I could go on and on about the deeper reasons for these kinds of hyperindipendence/control responses, from the poor governance from my country, to unreliable family dynamics (which tbh rn are better), to fucking idk today's hustle's culture. All things that converge into this pattern recognition of: the only thing I can do is trust myself. But if I have to be honest, the main reason I feel like this it's that for my past 3 relationships, especially the last one, I was always the one pushing to mantain them, not only that, I was always pushing to make myself heard, as well as the final push to end them.</p>
    <p>My past partners… I can't say that all of them were bad people, but none of them are people of which I would want a friendship with, and that tells me something about them. In a way I wasn't even a partner, just an additional supplement to their life, I don't think they knew a lot about me even when we were together, and in a way it's also my fault cause recieving from them felt wrong, but how do you open yourself up to someone so completely engulfed in themselves you know? Someone that doesn't live you space to be the best version of yourself but only the worst?</p>
    <p>I spent years chasing this version of love that never really belonged to me, basing its progress on other people's timelines. It was love, at least on my end, but it never felt like home, it felt like standing in the middle of a big house, full of perfect furniture no one has ever touched. Nothing out of place but nothing alive either, a space built but not at all lived in. And when I stopped asking myself how much of this was truly mine the question echoed into the house coming back unanswered.</p>
    <p>Luckly even when in the deepest trenches of this I never truly lost my life, I moved out of my country, continued my studies, my hobbies, my crafts, my friendships, but I know how much time and love I strippeded from myself trying to build something of which its fundations were simply not mine, staying in a house were I could rearrange the same furniture over and over and over and over, feeling at the very least like i could control something.</p>
    <p>I don't have the answer to this now, I think I can only learn by watching myself move more.</p>
